<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  globalsettings.commerce.fragment.config — the discount-engine activation block (Foundry #1048).

  THIS FILE IS A FRAGMENT, NOT A SETTINGS FILE. It is staged to
  Files/System/Truvio/ and read by nobody at runtime. It exists because the block below has
  to be MERGED into the host's own Files/GlobalSettings.config, and a layer's files/ overlay
  is overwrite-wins with no merge step: shipping a whole GlobalSettings.config from a layer
  would replace the host's database connection, mail server and licence configuration. So the
  distribution ships the exact block, in a file whose only job is to be copied out of.

  WHAT IT FIXES
  Two discount engines coexist in Dynamicweb 10. The legacy engine reads EcomDiscount
  (singular). The New Discount Experience - the one the admin Discounts screen renders, the
  one create_adjustment_discounts and its condition and reward verbs write - reads
  EcomDiscounts (plural), and reads it ONLY when this feature is activated. The baseline ships
  no activation block, so a solution can create a discount, condition it on a user group,
  reward it with a percentage, read it all back correctly, see DiscountActive true in the
  database, and have the cart charge full list price. Nothing in the chain reports it: there is
  no read that says the engine is dormant, and every tool returns success. Measured on the e2e
  host: three group net tiers at 32 / 18 / 40 percent, all three inert, while a customer-number
  contract price in the same cart resolved correctly - which makes it look like a discount
  configured wrong rather than an engine switched off.

  HOW TO APPLY IT
  1. Open the host's Files/GlobalSettings.config.
  2. Merge the Commerce element below into the existing <Features> element - merge, never
     replace: <Features> normally already carries Extranetnew, Capabilities, Dataintegration,
     Productsfeatures and Applicationfeatures blocks that other parts of the platform depend on.
     If there is no <Features> element at all, add one as a direct child of <Globalsettings>.
  3. Restart the host. The setting is read at startup; a running host keeps the dormant engine.
  4. The same switch is reachable in the admin at Settings > Administration > Feature Management,
     which is the route to prefer when a human is at the keyboard.

  HOW TO PROVE IT
  Not by reading the discount back. A read-back of a discount, its conditions and its rewards
  passes on a dormant engine, which is exactly how this went unnoticed. The only evidence is a
  rendered cart: sign in as a member of the conditioned group, add a product with a known list
  price, and read the line amount. PASS is the discounted amount in the cart body.

  WHY THIS LAYER CARRIES IT
  The base ships no GlobalSettings fragments at all - it is framework SQL, the contract and
  serializer config, nothing that touches host configuration files - so there is no established
  shape there to follow and inventing one on the privileged singleton for a single feature flag
  is the wrong trade. feature-pricing is the layer whose whole subject is price and discount
  resolution, so the precondition travels with it.
-->
<Globalsettings>
  <Features>
    <Commerce>
      <Newdiscountexperience>True</Newdiscountexperience>
    </Commerce>
  </Features>
</Globalsettings>
